The pentagon \(ABCDE\) has vertices in order with \(\angle A = \angle E = 90^\circ\) and \(\angle B = \angle D = 45^\circ\), so the remaining interior angle is \(\angle C = 540^\circ - 2\cdot90^\circ - 2\cdot45^\circ = 270^\circ\), making it nonconvex. Place \(A = (0,0)\) and \(E = (20,0)\) with \(AE = 20\). Since points \(B, C, D\) lie on the same side of line \(AE\), let \(B = (0,b)\) and \(D = (20,d)\) with \(b,d > 0\) and \(DE = d < AB = b\). The interior angles at \(B\) and \(D\) being \(45^\circ\) force the directions of \(BC\) and \(CD\) to be down‑right and up‑right respectively (or down‑left/up‑left, which leads to \(d > b\), contradicting \(d < b\)). This yields coordinates for \(C\) in terms of \(b\) and \(d\). Using \(BC = 14\sqrt{2}\) gives \(x_C = 14\), and solving for \(y_C\) gives the relations \(b = d + 8\) and \(y_C = d - 6\). The requirement that \(C\) is on the same side of \(AE\) as \(B\) and \(D\) forces \(d > 6\).

The area of the pentagon (using the shoelace formula) is \(20d - 4\). The problem requires the area to be an integer multiple of 16, so \(20d - 4 \equiv 0 \pmod{16} \Rightarrow 4d \equiv 4 \pmod{16} \Rightarrow d \equiv 1 \pmod{4}\). Write \(d = 4m + 1\) with integer \(m \ge 2\) (since \(d > 6\)). Then \(AB = b = d + 8 = 4m + 9\). The condition \(AB < 2026\) gives \(4m + 9 < 2026 \Rightarrow m \le 504\). The smallest allowed \(m\) is 2, so \(m \in \{2, 3, \dots, 504\}\), giving \(504 - 2 + 1 = 503\) possible values for \(AB\).

\boxed{503}
